%N A401905 Least m >= 0 such that k/2^n is a fusible number for every k >= m.
%C A401905 Fusible numbers (see A188545) are the times, counted from the start, that can be measured with fuses that each burn for one minute at an irregular rate, when a fuse end may be lit only at the start or when some fuse burns out, and no fuse is put out. They form the smallest set F containing 0 such that (x+y+1)/2 is in F whenever x and y are in F and |x-y| < 1.
%C A401905 For each n, only finitely many nonnegative multiples of 1/2^n are not fusible, yet a(n)/2^n tends to infinity (see Formula). The first fact is stated, and the second implied, in Xu's survey (Section 3, item 6).
%C A401905 For n >= 1, every fusible multiple of 1/2^n can be obtained from fusible multiples of 1/2^n alone, since each gap between consecutive fusible numbers is an interval (j/2^b, (j+1)/2^b); see Xu, Theorem 7, and Erickson, Nivasch and Xu, Lemmas 2.4 and 2.12 and Corollary 2.7. So for each n >= 1, a(n) is given by a finite computation on the grid of multiples of 1/2^n. With the bound a(n) <= (n-1)*2^(n-1) (see Formula), this decides whether k/2^n is fusible with at most (n-1)*4^(n-1) tests of pairs of grid points, whereas Erickson, Nivasch and Xu have no upper bound for the running time of their algorithms IsFusible and WeakSucc (Section 8).
%C A401905 For an integer k >= 0, let G(k) be the largest gap between consecutive fusible numbers in [k, infinity) (g(k) in Erickson, Nivasch and Xu). Then a(n) > k*2^n if and only if n >= 1 - log_2(G(k)), and 1 - log_2(G(k)) <= A188545(k) + 1. For k = 0, 1, 2 the least n with a(n) > k*2^n is 2, 4, 11, i.e., A188545(k) + 1.
%C A401905 Peano arithmetic proves that the grid computation halts on every input n, but it cannot prove that the resulting values a(n)/2^n are unbounded. This is a corollary of Theorems 1.2 and 6.1 of Erickson, Nivasch and Xu, the second being the theorem of Schwichtenberg and Wainer: the least n with a(n) > k*2^n, which is 1 - log_2(G(k)), eventually exceeds every function that Peano arithmetic proves to be total.
%C A401905 The least fusible number greater than 5/2 is 5/2 + 2^(-51), since a(52) = 5*2^51 + 2 gives x(52) = 5/2 (see Formula). This is the value predicted by the Main Conjecture in Xu's survey (Table 1).
%C A401905 a(n) = A027556(n+1) for n <= 10, but a(11) = 4098 while A027556(12) = 3906.
%C A401905 See the Ferudun link for proofs.
%H A401905 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A401905/b401905.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..120</a>
%H A401905 Jeff Erickson, Gabriel Nivasch and Junyan Xu, <a href="https://doi.org/10.46298/lmcs-18(3:6)2022">Fusible numbers and Peano Arithmetic</a>, Logical Methods in Computer Science, Vol. 18, No. 3 (2022), 6:1-6:26; arXiv:<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.14342">2003.14342</a> [cs.LO], 2020-2022.
%H A401905 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A401905/a401905.txt">Proofs of the comments and formulas</a>.
%H A401905 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A401905/a401905_1.txt">Rust program</a>.
%H A401905 Junyan Xu, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1202.5614">Survey on Fusible Numbers</a>, arXiv:1202.5614 [math.CO], 2012.
%F A401905 a(n) = 2^n*x(n) + 2 for n >= 2, where x(n) is the largest fusible number whose successor in F exceeds it by at least 2^(1-n). Then a(n)/2^n = x(n) + 2^(1-n) is the successor of x(n) in F. In the notation of Xu's survey, x(n) = g(n-1); Xu listed g(1..12), which gives a(2..13).
%F A401905 a(n) <= (n-1)*2^(n-1) for n >= 1, since every multiple of 1/2^n that is >= (n-1)/2 is fusible.
%F A401905 a(n) >= k*2^n + 2 for k >= 0 and n > A188545(k).
%e A401905 a(2) = 2: the only fusible number less than 1/2 is 0, so 1/4 is not fusible. Every multiple of 1/4 from 1/2 on is fusible, since 1/2 = (0+0+1)/2, 3/4 = (0+1/2+1)/2, and x + 1/2 = (x+x+1)/2 is fusible whenever x is.
%e A401905 a(11) = 4098: the least fusible number greater than 2 is 2 + 1/1024 (A188545(2) = 10), so 2 + 1/2048 = 4097/2048 is not fusible; it is the last multiple of 1/2048 that is not, since x(11) = g(10) = 2 (see Formula). As a(10) = 1922 < 2*2^10, n = 11 is the least n with a(n) > 2*2^n.
%o A401905 (Python)
%o A401905 from bisect import bisect_left
%o A401905 print(0)
%o A401905 for n in range(1, 19):
%o A401905     h = 1 << (n - 1)
%o A401905     f, members, streak = bytearray([1]), [0], 1
%o A401905     while streak < h:
%o A401905         k = len(f)
%o A401905         p = k >= h and f[k - h]
%o A401905         if not p and k >= h:
%o A401905             i = bisect_left(members, max(0, k - 2 * h + 1))
%o A401905             while i < len(members) and members[i] <= k - h:
%o A401905                 if f[2 * k - 2 * h - members[i]]:
%o A401905                     p = 1
%o A401905                     break
%o A401905                 i += 1
%o A401905         f.append(p)
%o A401905         if p:
%o A401905             members.append(k)
%o A401905         streak = streak + 1 if p else 0
%o A401905     print(len(f) - h)
%o A401905 (Rust) // See the Ferudun link "Rust program".
